          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and now I am not sleeping. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line, and is the insomnia treated separately?",
          "answer": "**Please reach out to someone today, and contact emergency services if you are in immediate danger.** The safety check flagged the highest urgency level. That flag came from the word \"suicidal\" in the check itself, not from anything you said. You did not tell me you have thoughts of harming yourself, but I still need to ask: **are you having any thoughts of suicide or of harming yourself?** If yes, or if you are not sure, call or text 988 in the United States, or your local crisis line. Many countries have an equivalent. You do not have to wait for a routine appointment. Also ask yourself whether you can still manage work, eating, washing, and daily tasks. If you cannot, or if the anxiety or sleeplessness is getting worse quickly, see a doctor or urgent care soon.\n\n**How anxiety is assessed.** A preventive-services and psychiatric guidance summary (*Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care*) describes the GAD-7, a seven-item questionnaire used to screen for and track severity. Scores of 5, 10 and 15 mark mild, moderate and severe, and 10 is the usual point for further assessment. That document says evidence for screening is strongest for adults under 65 and insufficient for older adults. A clinician makes the actual diagnosis. Generalized anxiety disorder is defined as persistent, hard-to-control worry on more days than not for at least six months, and disturbed sleep is among its commonly recognized features. Whether you meet that definition is for a clinician to decide. One source reported that it could not retrieve guidance on formal assessment, but the summary above comes from a separate search, so the GAD-7 information is available even though that search was incomplete.\n\n**First-line anxiety treatment.** The guidance I could retrieve does not clearly state the first-line treatment for anxiety. The same document says benzodiazepines are not first-line for generalized anxiety disorder and are unsuitable for long-term use. A clinician can explain the options.\n\n**Insomnia.** Guidance on chronic insomnia (*Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*) says cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia is first-line, ahead of medication, with effects that can last after treatment ends and fewer long-term harms. It also lists anxiety among conditions that often present as insomnia and should be screened for, and it describes a one-to-two-week sleep diary as part of assessment. Insomnia means trouble sleeping despite enough chance to sleep, with effects during the day. Short-term insomnia with a clear trigger is common and often settles. Chronic insomnia means at least three nights a week for at least three months. The retrieved passages do not say whether insomnia should be treated as a separate target alongside anxiety or as a symptom of it. That question is unresolved in what I could find, and I cannot report any disagreement between authorities on it.\n\n**Next step:** Book a visit with your doctor. Tell them about the months of anxiety, the new sleeplessness, and how it affects your daily life. If you have any thoughts of harming yourself, say so at the start.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
